A||||4||Gokart||Jinxed....32 back AGAIN.||||8||
Z||000000||Gokart||04-27-2000||09:32 PM||||Please help, I'm really losing my patience now with the engine light coming on and it always being 32. When it came on today I was PRAYING it would be something else this time. I just put in a brand new solenoid 2 weeks ago and put about 500 miles on it and she was running great. Had plenum off about 6 months ago and the tube checked out ok. I was able to check out vaccum lines at the same time. The valve seems to be fine as well cause it sputters and about stalls when I push up on the diaphram. The light came on today when I got her going about 80 mph and stayed on till I got to work. On the way home I was only going a gentle 65 and it didn't come on at all. I'm thinking it could be a vaccum leak around the plenum but when I spray around it there is no fluxuation in the idle.......HELP!||||reg||8
Z||000001||Raydar||04-28-2000||12:56 AM||||GoKart, I feel your pain.<BR>Six months after spending $102 for a solenoid from GM, my code 32 started returning. Dug out the service manual and went through the procedure. Damned new solenoid went bad. Actually only *half* bad.<BR>The large cylinder-shaped device is actually the solenoid. It responds to the signal from the ECM, and switches engine vacuum to the EGR valve. The square box that clips to the side is a sensor. It detects the presence of vacuum (which is controlled by the solenoid), and provides an output (contact closure) to the ECM. I ended up taking the sensor off of my new (defective) solenoid and connecting it to my old solenoid (which had a bad sensor). Had to cut and splice the wires, but it's worked flawlessly, ever since. Used a cheap ohm-meter to make sure I had the wires spliced in the correct order. If you can obtain a hand-operated vacuum pump, it will make the EGR system infinitely easier to troubleshoot. They are about $30-35 at Advance Auto.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Gokart||04-28-2000||05:29 AM||||I'll pick one up this weekend. Thanks Raydar! I also have a cheap ohm meter. How do I check wires for the EGR system? Can I touch the prongs in the plug that goes to the solenoid and get some sort of reading? Also, what is the best cleaner/chemical I could use if I decide to pull the plenum again this weekend. I'm thinking that because of the black rock that formed around the hole where the tube meets the plenum the 1st time I pulled it there could be more of this crap causing a restriction of air. What could I soak the tube and plenum in to get it all cleaned out? TIA for reply!<P><p>[This message has been edited by Gokart (edited 04-28-2000).]||||reg||2
Z||000003||triker||04-28-2000||11:48 AM||||I've had trouble with my EGR solenoid also.  Unless you have to have regular smog certification,  who wants an EGR valve?  I finally hooked the vacumn line that operates the solenoid directly to the switch (little square box with short vacumn line to it), and plugged the vent line.  This fools the ECM and stops the EGR from opening.  No more service soon light!||||reg||1
Z||000004||mshill||04-28-2000||12:34 PM||||Gokart,  mine does the exact same thing.  I have cleaned the switch contacts, blown the filter out, checked the EGR valve and vacuum lines with a vacuum pump.  Mine has come on only twice in the last 400 miles and it seems to be above 75 mph as well and usually on a warmer day.  My EGR tube is next.<P>||||reg||1
